Bloch-decomposition based time-splitting spectral algorithm
\label{sec:2.2}
If we consider an external potential $V_{ext}\(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\)$ in the equation \eqref{eq000} \cite{Fri,MT},
we have
\[
i\varepsilon\partial_t\psi=(i\varepsilon\nabla-A)^2\psi+V_{ext}(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})\psi+c\frac{1}{|\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}|}\psi+C_1(|\cdot|^{-1}\ast|\psi|^2)\psi
-a|\psi|^\sigma\psi,
\]
by the same transformation \eqref{eqlenrd} and by fixing $\varepsilon=1$, we obtain the following equation
\begin{equation}\label{ext-fast}
i\partial_tu^\omega=-\Delta u^\omega+V_{ext}\(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}-b(t)\)u^\omega+V^\omega u
+C_1(|\cdot|^{-1}\ast|u^\omega|^2)u^\omega-a|u^\omega|^\sigma u^\omega,
\end{equation}
and its time-averaged problem is
\begin{equation}\label{ext-avr}
i\partial_tu=-\Delta u+\langle V_{ext}\rangle u+\langle V\rangle u+C_1(|\cdot|^{-1}\ast|u|^2)u-a|u|^\sigma u,
\end{equation}
with
\[\langle V_{ext}\rangle(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}) = \displaystyle\int\limits_{-\frac{1}2}^{{\frac{1}2}} V_{ext}\left(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}-e(t) \sin(2\pi  s)\right) ds.\]


In particular, if we consider a case with an periodic potential $V_\Gamma\(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\)$ \cite{Fri,MT},
{\sl i.e.\/ } $V_\Gamma(\boldsymbol{\rm{y}})$ is periodic w.r.t to a regular lattice $\Gamma$,
\[V_\Gamma(\boldsymbol{\rm{y}}+\gamma)=V_\Gamma(\boldsymbol{\rm{y}}), \quad \forall \gamma\in\Gamma, \boldsymbol{\rm{y}}\in \mathbb{R}^3,\]
the above two equations \eqref{ext-fast}--\eqref{ext-avr} become
\begin{equation}\label{bloch-fast}
i\partial_tu^\omega=-\Delta u^\omega+V_\Gamma\(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}-b(t)\)u^\omega+V^\omega u
+C_1(|\cdot|^{-1}\ast|u^\omega|^2)u^\omega-a|u^\omega|^\sigma u^\omega,
\end{equation}
and its time-averaged problem is
\begin{equation}\label{bloch-avr}
i\partial_tu=-\Delta u+\langle V_\Gamma\rangle\(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\) u+\langle V\rangle u+C_1(|\cdot|^{-1}\ast|u|^2)u-a|u|^\sigma u,
\end{equation}
with
\[\langle V_{\Gamma}\rangle(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}) = \displaystyle\int\limits_{-\frac{1}2}^{{\frac{1}2}} V_{\Gamma}\left(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}-e(t) \sin(2\pi  s)\right) ds.\]

We solve \eqref{bloch-fast} or \eqref{bloch-avr} by using the \emph{Bloch-decomposition based time-splitting method}, see
\cite{HJMS, HJMS-2}.


\textbf{Step 1.} We solve the equation
\begin{equation}
\label{bd1}
\begin{aligned}
&\, \mathrm{i} \partial _t u_1 = -\, \Delta u_1 +V_\Gamma\(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\) u_1, \\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
on a fixed time interval $\Delta t$, relying on the
\emph{Bloch-decomposition based pseudo-spectral} method.

Let $\rho(t^{n+1},\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})=|u_1(t^{n+1},\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})|^2$, then we obtain the Hartree potential $V_{Hartree}$ by
\[V_{Hartree}\(t^{n+1},\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\)=\(\frac{1}{|\xi|^2}\hat{\rho}(t^{n+1}, \xi)\)^\vee.\]

\textbf{Step 2.} Then, we solve the ordinary differential equation
\begin{equation}
\label{bd2}
\begin{aligned}
& \,\mathrm{i} \partial _tu_2  = \left(C_1V_{Hartree}+V(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})-a|u_2|^\sigma\right)u_2, \quad u_2\(t^{n},\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\)=u_1\(t^{n+1},\boldsymbol{\rm{x}}\),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
on the same time-interval, here $V(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})$ is given by \eqref{eq:fast-p} or \eqref{eq:avr-p}.
It is easy to check that  in this step the density $\rho(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})\equiv|u_2|^2(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})$ and consequently the Hartree potential do not change in time, hence
\[ u_2(t+\Delta t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})=u_2(t,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})\,\exp\({\displaystyle-\mathrm{i} \int_{t}^{t+\Delta t}
\(C_1V_{Hartree}(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})+V(s,\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})-a\rho^\frac{\sigma}{2}(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})\) ds}\)
\] with $\rho(\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})=\left|u_1(t^{n+1},\boldsymbol{\rm{x}})\right|^2$, $V_{Hartree}(x)=\(\frac{1}{|\xi|^2}\hat{\rho}(\xi)\)^\vee$.


\begin{remark} Certainly, if there is no lattice potential $V_\Gamma$, the above two algorithms are coincided with each other.
But we must include the lattice potential $V_\Gamma$ when the interaction with laser or other lattice potenital are not eligible.
We will see that the Bloch-decomposition based method is much more efficient in the case with lattice potential $V_\Gamma$
than the traditional pseudo-spectral method ({\sl cf.\/ } Fig. \ref{Fig012}-\ref{Fig013}), {\sl i.e.\/ } we could use much larger mesh size
than the traditional pseudo-spectral method to get the same accuracy.
\end{remark}

